The rewrite is the plugin's local detection engine as a Nodecode cell. What it
keeps, rule for rule and from the plugin's own tables: the seven built-in
prompt-injection heuristics with their severities and OWASP categories
(detection/content_scanners.py); the secret-VALUE shapes -- private-key
blocks, the provider keys, a labelled wallet key, a service-account file, a
JWT (kernel/redaction.py); the shell shapes that stand on their own -- a
download piped into a shell, rm -rf of a root or home, chmod 777, a fetch with
certificate checks off, sudo (detection/shell_shapes.py); the closed severity
scale and the audit/block wording; the default protected paths and the idea
that reading one is a finding of its own (kernel/config.py); and the egress
rule that makes a secret in a command that sends data the worst case
(detection/secrets_handled/sending.py). The model-facing tool descriptions
and the manual keep the plugin's own vocabulary: never the prompt text, only
the pattern's identifier and the matched phrase.

What is left out is the half that needs the network: the threat graph the
plugin syncs from a local OriginTrail DKG node, the curated and community
rules it adds to the built-in ones, the OSV lookup of an installed package,
the optional LLM second opinion, and the community report the plugin shares
(with its signing keys, keep-alive epochs and dashboards). A cell cannot sync
a graph it does not have, and a detection engine that is honest about what it
knows is more useful than one that claims a graph it cannot reach. The finer
crypto-wallet shapes (Solana seed halves, Bitcoin WIF, seed phrases proven by
checksum) are left out with them: what remains is the labelled-hex shape.

The plugin hooks every tool call and model request and can block; this cell
is called on purpose, by the model through two tools and by the operator
through /blackbox, and it blocks nothing. The `mode` setting keeps the
plugin's two words for what a finding means.

Nothing of the original's Python is bundled: the rules are data in cell.lisp
and the cell's own code is Common Lisp.
